No nib name means no nib, not any nib.

-[NSWindowController windowNibPath] asked the bundle for a nib WITHOUT CHECKING THAT IT HAD A NAME
TO ASK FOR. pathForResource:nil ofType: is not a failed lookup on macOS, it is a request for the
first resource of that type in the bundle, and it answers one. So a controller with no nib name
did not decline to load a nib: it loaded whichever nib happened to come first, with itself as the
File's Owner.

MoneyMoney's PreferencesWindowController has no nib name, and Preferences never opened. Measured
before, with CIDER_TRACE_CONTROL and a spy on the two accessors:

    PreferencesWindowController windowNibName -> (nil)
    PreferencesWindowController windowNibPath -> /Applications/MoneyMoney.app/Contents/Resources/TemplateGroupView.nib
    CIDER_WC PreferencesWindowController loadNibFile enter
    CIDER_WC PreferencesWindowController adopted window=0x0 controller=0x0

and after:

    PreferencesWindowController windowNibName -> (nil)
    PreferencesWindowController windowNibPath -> (nil)
    (no loadNibFile for that controller at all)

MainWindowController is the control in both runs and is unaffected: it names MainWindow and still
resolves it.

A nib loaded against the wrong owner is a silent wrong answer of the shape this port keeps
finding. It runs awakeFromNib on objects the controller never asked for and offers its outlets to
connections meant for another owner, and nothing in it raises. This does not by itself open
MoneyMoney's Preferences, which is blocked separately by a caught unrecognized selector, so the
fix is recorded on its own terms rather than as the cure for that window.

Task #254.

--- a/AppKit/NSWindowController.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSWindowController.m
@@ -408,8 +408,19 @@
         return _nibPath;
     else {
         NSString *name = [self windowNibName];
-        NSBundle *bundle = [NSBundle bundleForClass: [_owner class]];
-        NSString *path = [bundle pathForResource: name ofType: @"nib"];
+        NSBundle *bundle;
+        NSString *path;
+
+        /* NO NAME MEANS NO NIB, NOT ANY NIB. pathForResource:nil answers the FIRST resource of
+         * that type in the bundle, so a controller with no nib name loaded an arbitrary one with
+         * itself as File's Owner: MoneyMoney's PreferencesWindowController loaded
+         * TemplateGroupView.nib, adopted no window from it, and lost its Preferences window to the
+         * connections that nib made instead. Task #254. */
+        if ([name length] == 0) {
+            return nil;
+        }
+        bundle = [NSBundle bundleForClass: [_owner class]];
+        path = [bundle pathForResource: name ofType: @"nib"];

         if (path == nil)
             path = [[NSBundle mainBundle] pathForResource: name ofType: @"nib"];
